window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-37"]=[{"id":"q4_cont_142","q":"4:142–143","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:142–143 · Hypocrisy in prayer and remembrance","source":"Cairo I:578","cairo":"I:578–580","source_page_first":578,"source_page_last":580,"ar":"إِنَّ الْمُنافِقِينَ يُخادِعُونَ اللَّهَ وَهُوَ خادِعُهُمْ وَإِذا قامُوا إِلَى الصَّلاةِ قامُوا كُسالى يُراؤُنَ النَّاسَ وَلا يَذْكُرُونَ اللَّهَ إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً (142) مُذَبْذَبِينَ بَيْنَ ذلِكَ لآَ إِلى هؤُلاءِ وَلا إِلى هؤُلاءِ وَمَنْ يُضْلِلِ اللَّهُ فَلَنْ تَجِدَ لَهُ سَبِيلاً (143)\nقلت: جملة: (ولا يذكرون الله) حال من واو (يُراءون) ، وكذلك (مذبذبين) أي: يراءون حال كونهم غير ذاكرين مذبذبين، أو منصوب على الذم، والمذبذب: المضطرب المتردد.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ الْمُنافِقِينَ يُخادِعُونَ اللَّهَ\nبإظهار الإيمان وإخفاء الكفر، وَهُوَ خادِعُهُمْ\n، أي: مجازيهم على خداعهم بأن يظهر لهم يوم القيامة نورًا يمشون به على الصراط، كما يعطي المؤمنين، فإذا مضوا به طفئ نورهم وبقي نور المؤمنين، فينادونهم: انْظُرُونا نَقْتَبِسْ مِنْ نُورِكُمْ قِيلَ ارْجِعُوا وَراءَكُمْ فَالْتَمِسُوا نُوراً، فيتهافتون في النار. فسمي هذه العقوبة خداعًا تسمية للعقوبة باسم الذنب.\nوكانوا إِذا قامُوا إِلَى الصَّلاةِ قامُوا كُسالى\nأي: متثاقلين، لا يريدون بها وجه الله، فإن رءاهم أحد، صلوا، وإلاَّ انصرفوا، فلم يصلوا، يُراؤُنَ\nبأعمالهم النَّاسَ\nأي: المؤمنين، وَلا يَذْكُرُونَ اللَّهَ إِلَّا قَلِيلًا\nلأن المرائى لا يذكر إلا بحضرة الناس، وهو أقل أحواله، أو لا يذكرونه في صلاتهم إلا قليلاً، لأنهم لا يذكرون إلا التكبير والتسليم، وقال ابن عباس: إنما ذلك لأنهم يفعلونها رياءً وسمعةً، ولو أرادوا بذلك وجه الله تعالى لكان كثيرًا.\nوقال قتادة: إنما قل ذكرهم، لأنه لم يُقبل، فكل ما رُدَّ من العمل فهو قليل، وكل ما قُبل فهو كثير.\nوكانوا أيضًا مُذَبْذَبِينَ أي: مترددين ومتحيرين بين الكفر والإيمان، لا إِلى هؤُلاءِ وَلا إِلى هؤُلاءِ أي: لا صائرين إلى المؤمنين ولا إلى الكافرين. قال قتادة: ما هم بمؤمنين مخلصين، ولا مشركين مُصَرِّحين بالشرك، هكذا سبق في علم الله، وَمَنْ يُضْلِلِ اللَّهُ فَلَنْ تَجِدَ لَهُ سَبِيلًا أي: طريقًا إلى الهدى، ومثله قوله تعالى: وَمَنْ لَمْ يَجْعَلِ اللَّهُ لَهُ نُوراً فَما لَهُ مِنْ نُورٍ.\nالإشارة: كل من أحب أن يَرى الناسُ محاسنَ أعماله وأحواله، ففيه شعبة من النفاق وشعبة من الرياء، وعلامة المرائي: تزيين ظاهره وتخريب باطنه، يتزين للناس بحسن أعماله وأحواله، يراقب الناس ولا يراقب الله، وكان بعضُ الحكماء يقول: يقول الله- تعالى-: «يا مُرائي: أمرُ من ترائي بيد من تعصيه» . فمثل هذا أعماله كلها قليلة، ولو كثرت في الحس كالجبال الرواسي، وأعمال المخلصين كلها كثيرة ولو قلَّت في الحس، وأعمال المرائين كلها قليلة ولو كثرت في الحس. قال في القوت: وَصَفَ اللهُ تعالى ذكر المنافقين بالقلة، لكونه غير خالص، كما قيل في تفسير قوله تعالى: ذِكْراً كَثِيراً أي: خالصًا، فسمي الخالص كثيرا. هـ.\nقوله تعالى: مُذَبْذَبِينَ بَيْنَ ذلِكَ: هذه صفة أهل الدعوى، المستشرفين على الحقيقة بالعلم، ليسوا من الخصوص ولا من العموم، مترددين بين الفريقين، ومن يضلل الله عن طريق التحقيق، فلن تجد له سبيلا.\nثم نهى المؤمنين عن موالاة الكفار لئلا يتشبهوا بالمنافقين، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ الْمُنافِقِينَ يُخادِعُونَ اللَّهَ وَهُوَ خادِعُهُمْ وَإِذا قامُوا إِلَى الصَّلاةِ قامُوا كُسالى يُراؤُنَ النَّاسَ وَلا يَذْكُرُونَ اللَّهَ إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً (142) مُذَبْذَبِينَ بَيْنَ ذلِكَ لآَ إِلى هؤُلاءِ وَلا إِلى هؤُلاءِ وَمَنْ يُضْلِلِ اللَّهُ فَلَنْ تَجِدَ لَهُ سَبِيلاً (143)","quran_en":"The hypocrites attempt to deceive God, while He turns their deception against them. When they stand for prayer, they stand reluctantly, showing themselves to people and remembering God only a little—wavering between the two groups, belonging wholly neither to these nor to those. Whoever God leads astray, for him you will find no path.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: “They remember God only a little” is circumstantial to the subject of “they display themselves,” as is “wavering”: they make a show while not truly remembering, suspended between alternatives. Alternatively “wavering” may be accusative in censure. <em>Mudhadhdhab<\/em> denotes one disturbed, oscillating and unable to settle.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “They remember God only a little” is circumstantial to the subject of “they display themselves,” as is “wavering”: they make a show while not truly remembering, suspended between alternatives. Alternatively “wavering” may be accusative in censure. Mudhadhdhab denotes one disturbed, oscillating and unable to settle."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>The hypocrites profess faith and conceal unbelief. God is said to “deceive” them in the sense of a corresponding punishment, named after their act: in the hereafter they are shown a light to walk by upon the bridge, as the faithful are; when they move on, the hypocrites' light is extinguished while the believers' light remains, and they call for light before falling into punishment (cf. 57:13). They undertake prayer lazily because God is not their intention. If watched, they pray; if unseen, they abandon it. They seek reputation by their actions and remember God only when observed, which occupies the smaller part of their time. Or remembrance within their prayer is slight, restricted to the opening and closing formulas without inward attention. Ibn ʿAbbās says their act is designed for display and reputation: if performed for God it would be much remembrance. Qatāda says rejected remembrance is small no matter how abundant its appearance; accepted remembrance is great even if externally slight. Such hypocrites oscillate between denial and faith, genuine believers and open polytheists, at ease with neither. God has known their state, and whoever is left astray has no other guide, like the verse, “Whoever God gives no light has no light” (24:40).<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The hypocrites profess faith and conceal unbelief. God is said to “deceive” them in the sense of a corresponding punishment, named after their act: in the hereafter they are shown a light to walk by upon the bridge, as the faithful are; when they move on, the hypocrites' light is extinguished while the believers' light remains, and they call for light before falling into punishment (cf. 57:13). They undertake prayer lazily because God is not their intention. If watched, they pray; if unseen, they abandon it. They seek reputation by their actions and remember God only when observed, which occupies the smaller part of their time. Or remembrance within their prayer is slight, restricted to the opening and closing formulas without inward attention. Ibn ʿAbbās says their act is designed for display and reputation: if performed for God it would be much remembrance. Qatāda says rejected remembrance is small no matter how abundant its appearance; accepted remembrance is great even if externally slight. Such hypocrites oscillate between denial and faith, genuine believers and open polytheists, at ease with neither. God has known their state, and whoever is left astray has no other guide, like the verse, “Whoever God gives no light has no light” (24:40)."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>A desire that people should admire one's good deeds or spiritual states contains a branch of hypocrisy and ostentation (<em>riyāʾ<\/em>). Its mark is decorating the outward appearance while neglecting the inner state, attending to people rather than God. One of the sages used to say, “God Most High says: ‘You who show off, the affairs of those before whom you show off are in the hands of the One you disobey.’” All his outwardly gigantic deeds are inwardly small; the sincere person's little work is great. <em>Al-Qūt<\/em> is cited for understanding abundant remembrance as pure remembrance, for sincerity makes it abundant. “Wavering” also describes mere claimants to realisation who glimpse inward truth intellectually but belong fully neither to the special people nor to ordinary religious society. Without divine guidance onto the path of actual verification (<em>taḥqīq<\/em>), they have no way to its goal.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"A desire that people should admire one's good deeds or spiritual states contains a branch of hypocrisy and ostentation ( riyāʾ ). Its mark is decorating the outward appearance while neglecting the inner state, attending to people rather than God. One of the sages used to say, “God Most High says: ‘You who show off, the affairs of those before whom you show off are in the hands of the One you disobey.’” All his outwardly gigantic deeds are inwardly small; the sincere person's little work is great. Al-Qūt is cited for understanding abundant remembrance as pure remembrance, for sincerity makes it abundant. “Wavering” also describes mere claimants to realisation who glimpse inward truth intellectually but belong fully neither to the special people nor to ordinary religious society. Without divine guidance onto the path of actual verification ( taḥqīq ), they have no way to its goal."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2478,"arabic_characters":2624},{"id":"q4_cont_144","q":"4:144","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:144 · Taking unbelief as patrons","source":"Cairo I:580","cairo":"I:580","source_page_first":580,"source_page_last":580,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَتَّخِذُوا الْكافِرِينَ أَوْلِياءَ مِنْ دُونِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ أَتُرِيدُونَ أَنْ تَجْعَلُوا لِلَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ سُلْطاناً مُبِيناً (144)\nقلت: اتخذ، يتعدّى إلى مفعولين، و (من دون) : حال.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تتشبهوا بالمنافقين فتتخذوا الْكافِرِينَ أَوْلِياءَ وأصدقاء مِنْ دُونِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ لأن الله أعزكم بالإيمان والنصر، فلا تطلبوا العز من أحد سواه، أَتُرِيدُونَ أَنْ تَجْعَلُوا لِلَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ سُلْطاناً مُبِيناً أي: حجة واضحة على تعذيبكم وسببًا في عقابكم.\nالإشارة: قد تقدم في كثير من الإشارات النهي عن موالاة أهل الإنكار على الأولياء، وعن مخالطة أهل الدنيا وصحبتهم، فإن ذلك حجة واضحة على الرجوع إليهم ومصانعتهم، وهو عين النفاق عند المخلصين.\nوالله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر وعيد المنافقين، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَتَّخِذُوا الْكافِرِينَ أَوْلِياءَ مِنْ دُونِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ أَتُرِيدُونَ أَنْ تَجْعَلُوا لِلَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ سُلْطاناً مُبِيناً (144)","quran_en":"O believers, do not take unbelievers as your patrons instead of believers. Do you wish to give God an unmistakable argument against you?","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>ittakhadha<\/em> governs two objects, and “instead of” is circumstantial. Do not imitate the hypocrites by seeking protection and friendship through enemies of the believing community, as though worldly power were the source of dignity. God has already ennobled believers through faith and victory. Such a choice would provide manifest evidence for their censure and punishment.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: ittakhadha governs two objects, and “instead of” is circumstantial. Do not imitate the hypocrites by seeking protection and friendship through enemies of the believing community, as though worldly power were the source of dignity. God has already ennobled believers through faith and victory. Such a choice would provide manifest evidence for their censure and punishment."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Ibn ʿAjība reiterates his warning against alliance with those who denounce God's saints and immersion in companionship devoted to worldly ambition. This is clear evidence of returning to them and ingratiating oneself with them; for sincere seekers that split allegiance is a form of inward hypocrisy. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Ibn ʿAjība reiterates his warning against alliance with those who denounce God's saints and immersion in companionship devoted to worldly ambition. This is clear evidence of returning to them and ingratiating oneself with them; for sincere seekers that split allegiance is a form of inward hypocrisy. God knows best."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":695,"arabic_characters":759},{"id":"q4_cont_145","q":"4:145–147","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:145–147 · The lowest depth and the opening of repentance","source":"Cairo I:580","cairo":"I:580–582","source_page_first":580,"source_page_last":582,"ar":"إِنَّ الْمُنافِقِينَ فِي الدَّرْكِ الْأَسْفَلِ مِنَ النَّارِ وَلَنْ تَجِدَ لَهُمْ نَصِيراً (145) إِلاَّ الَّذِينَ تابُوا وَأَصْلَحُوا وَاعْتَصَمُوا بِاللَّهِ وَأَخْلَصُوا دِينَهُمْ لِلَّهِ فَأُولئِكَ مَعَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ وَسَوْفَ يُؤْتِ اللَّهُ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ أَجْراً عَظِيماً (146) مَّا يَفْعَلُ اللَّهُ بِعَذابِكُمْ إِنْ شَكَرْتُمْ وَآمَنْتُمْ وَكانَ اللَّهُ شاكِراً عَلِيماً (147)\nقلت: الدَّرَك: والدَّرْك لغتان، كالظَّعَن والظَّعْن، والنَّهَر والنَّهْر، والنَّشَر والنَّشْر، وهي الطبقة السفلى، وسميت طبقاتهم دركات لأنها مُتداركة متتابعة، وهي ضد الدرجات، فالدرجات للعلو، والدركات للسفل.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ الْمُنافِقِينَ فِي الدَّرْكِ الْأَسْفَلِ مِنَ النَّارِ أي: في الطبقة السفلى في قَعر جهنم لأنهم أخبث الكفرة، حيث ضموا إلى الكفر الاستهزاء بالإسلام وخداع المسلمين. قال ابن مسعود رضي الله عنه:\n(هم في توابيت من النار مقفلة عليهم في النار، مطبقة عليهم) . وعن ابن عمر رضي الله عنه: (إن أشدُّ الناس عذاباً يومَ القيامة ثلاثة: المنافقون، ومن كفر من أصحاب المائدة، وآل فرعون لقوله: إِنَّ الْمُنافِقِينَ فِي الدَّرْكِ الْأَسْفَلِ\nمِنَ النَّارِ\nوقال في أصحاب المائدة: فَإِنِّي أُعَذِّبُهُ عَذاباً لآ أُعَذِّبُهُ أَحَداً مِنَ الْعالَمِينَ. وقال: أَدْخِلُوا آلَ فِرْعَوْنَ أَشَدَّ الْعَذابِ، وَلَنْ تَجِدَ لَهُمْ نَصِيراً يمنعهم من ذلك العذاب. إِلَّا الَّذِينَ تابُوا عن النفاق وَأَصْلَحُوا ما أفسدوا في سرائرهم وأعمالهم في حال النفاق، وَاعْتَصَمُوا بِاللَّهِ أي: وثقوا به وتمسكوا به، دون أحد سواه، وَأَخْلَصُوا دِينَهُمْ لِلَّهِ لا يريدون بطاعته إلا وجه الله، لا رياء ولا سمعة فَأُولئِكَ مَعَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ في الدين. قال الفرَّاء: من المؤمنين، وقال العتبي: حاد عن كلامهم غيظًا عليهم، ولم يقل هم المؤمنون. هـ. قلت:\nإنما قال: مَعَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ ولم يقل: منهم، لأنَّ التخلص من النفاق صعب، ولا يكون من المؤمنين، حتى يتخلص من جميع شعبه، وهو عزيز، وقد قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «ثلاث من كن فيه فهُوَ مُنَافقٌ، وإن صَامَ وصلَّى وزَعم أنه مُسلمٌ، من إذا حدَّثَ كَذَبَ، وإذا وعد أخلف، وإذا ائُتمِنَ خَانَ» .\nوَسَوْفَ يُؤْتِ اللَّهُ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ المخلصين أَجْراً عَظِيماً فيساهُمونَهم فيه إن تابوا وأصلحوا، فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ غَنِيٌّ عَنِ عذابهم، ولذلك قال: مَّا يَفْعَلُ اللَّهُ بِعَذابِكُمْ إِنْ شَكَرْتُمْ وَآمَنْتُمْ أي: لا حاجة له في عذابكم، فلا يشفى به غيظًا ولا يدفع به ضررًا، أو يستجلب به نفعًا لأنه غنيَّ عن المنافع، وإنما يعاقب المصر بكفره، لأن إصراره عليه كسوء المزاج يؤدي إلى مرض، فإن زال بالإيمان والشكر، ونقَّى منه قلبه، تخلص من تبعته. وإنما قدم الشكر لأن الناظر يدرك النعم أولاً فيشكر شكرًا مبهمًا، ثم يمعن النظر حتى يعرف المنعم فيؤمن به. قاله البيضاوي. وقال الثعلبي: فيه تقديم وتأخير، أي إن آمنتم وشكرتم، لأن الشكر لا ينفع مع عدم الإيمان. وَكانَ اللَّهُ شاكِراً لأعمال عباده، يقبل اليسير ويعطي الكثير، عَلِيماً بحقيقة شكرهم وإيمانهم، ومقدار أعمالهم، فيضاعفها على قدر تخليصها. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: لا شيء أصعب على النفس من الإخلاص كلما اجتهد العبد في قطع الرياء نبت على لون آخر، فلا يتطهر العبد منها إلا بتحقيق الفناء والغيبة عن السوى بالكلية. كما قال الششترى رضي الله عنه:\nطهِّر العَينَ بالمَدامِعِ سَكبًا ... مِن شُهُودِ السَّوى تَزُل كلُّ عِلَّه\nقال بعضهم: [لا ينبت الإخلاص في القلب حتى يَسقط من عين الناس، ويُسقط الناسَ من عينه] . والإخلاص من أعمال القلوب، فلا يطَّلع عليه إلا علام الغيوب. فلا يجوز أن يحكم على أحد بالرياء بمجرد ما يرى عليه من الإظهار، وقد تدخل الرياء مع الإسرار، وتتخلص من القلب مع الإظهار، وفي الحكم: «ربما دخل الرياءُ عليك حيث لا ينظر الخلق إليك» . فإذا تخلص العبد من دقائق الرياء، وأصلح ما بينه وبين الله، واعتصم به دون شيء سواه، كان مع المخلصين المقربين فيكون عمله موفورًا، وسعيه مشكورًا. وبالله التوفيق.\nوقد تكلم في الإحياء على هذه الآية فقال: إنما كان المنافقون في الدرك الأسفل لأنهم جحدوا بعد العلم، وإنما تضاعف عذاب العالم في معصيته لأنه عَصَى عن علم. قلت: وافهم منه قوله صلّى الله عليه وسلم في أبي طالب: «وَلَولاَ أنَا لَكَانَ فِي الدَّركِ الأسفلَ مِنَ النَّارِ» . وذلك لإعراضه مع العلم. وقال في الإحياء أيضًا: شدَّد أمر المنافقين لأن الكافر كفرَ وأظهَرَ، والمنافق كفر وستر، فكان ستره لكفره كفرًا آخر، لأنه استخف بنظر الله إلى قلبه، وعظَّم أمر المخلوقين. هـ. والحاصل: أن التشديد في الرياء والنفاق لِمَا في ذلك من تعظيم نظر الخلق على نظر الخالق، فكان أعظمَ من الكفر الصريح. هـ. من الحاشية.\nومن علامة تصفية الباطن من الرياء والنفاق تلبس الظاهر بأحسن الأخلاق، ولذلك ذكره بِأَثَره، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ الْمُنافِقِينَ فِي الدَّرْكِ الْأَسْفَلِ مِنَ النَّارِ وَلَنْ تَجِدَ لَهُمْ نَصِيراً (145) إِلاَّ الَّذِينَ تابُوا وَأَصْلَحُوا وَاعْتَصَمُوا بِاللَّهِ وَأَخْلَصُوا دِينَهُمْ لِلَّهِ فَأُولئِكَ مَعَ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ وَسَوْفَ يُؤْتِ اللَّهُ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ أَجْراً عَظِيماً (146) مَّا يَفْعَلُ اللَّهُ بِعَذابِكُمْ إِنْ شَكَرْتُمْ وَآمَنْتُمْ وَكانَ اللَّهُ شاكِراً عَلِيماً (147)","quran_en":"Hypocrites are in the lowest depth of the Fire; no helper will you find for them—except those who repent, repair their conduct, hold fast to God and make their religion sincerely His. They are then with the believers, whom God shall grant a tremendous reward. Why would God punish you if you are thankful and have faith? God appreciates and knows.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>darak<\/em> and <em>dark<\/em> are two vocalisations, like <em>ẓaʿan/ẓaʿn<\/em>, <em>nahar/nahr<\/em>, and <em>nashar/nashr<\/em>. <em>Darakāt<\/em> are the successive descending layers, in contrast with rising <em>darajāt<\/em>. Hypocrites occupy the lowest depth because their denial was compounded by ridicule and deliberate deception of believers. Ibn Masʿūd is reported to describe them as locked in coffins of Fire. Ibn ʿUmar is quoted: “The three kinds of people punished most severely on the Day of Resurrection are the hypocrites, those of the People of the Table who disbelieved, and Pharaoh’s people,” citing: “The hypocrites are in the lowest depth of the Fire” [4:145]; concerning the People of the Table, “I shall punish him with a punishment with which I punish no other being” [5:115]; and “Admit Pharaoh’s people to the severest punishment” [40:46]. <em>[Translator’s note: These descriptions of the unseen are presented in the source as reports.]<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The exception is real. Those who repent of hypocrisy, correct outward deeds and inward dispositions, trust in and attach themselves to God alone, and purify worship of ostentation and the wish for reputation, are joined to believers. Al-Farrāʾ takes “with the believers” as meaning “among the believers,” while al-ʿUtbī supposes the wording deliberately avoids saying “they are believers” to indicate God's displeasure. Ibn ʿAjība responds: “He says ‘with the believers,’ not ‘from among them,’ because escape from hypocrisy is difficult. One does not belong among the believers until freed from all its branches, and this is rare.” He cites the familiar Prophetic warning about lying when speaking, breaking promises and betraying trusts, even if a person fasts, prays and asserts Islam.<\/p>\n<p>Sincere people receive great reward, and the repentant share in it. God has no need to inflict punishment for personal gratification, since He is not harmed by sin or benefited by worship. Persisting in disbelief produces a spiritual disease; faith and thanksgiving cure it. Al-Bayḍāwī explains that gratitude precedes faith in the verse because a person first experiences benefits and offers rudimentary gratitude, then reflects until recognising the Giver. Al-Thaʿlabī instead takes the sequence as transposed: belief precedes gratitude, since gratitude without faith brings no saving benefit. God is <em>Shākir<\/em>, accepting slight deeds and repaying them many times over, and <em>ʿAlīm<\/em>, knowing the true degree of sincerity.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: darak and dark are two vocalisations, like ẓaʿan/ẓaʿn , nahar/nahr , and nashar/nashr . Darakāt are the successive descending layers, in contrast with rising darajāt . Hypocrites occupy the lowest depth because their denial was compounded by ridicule and deliberate deception of believers. Ibn Masʿūd is reported to describe them as locked in coffins of Fire. Ibn ʿUmar is quoted: “The three kinds of people punished most severely on the Day of Resurrection are the hypocrites, those of the People of the Table who disbelieved, and Pharaoh’s people,” citing: “The hypocrites are in the lowest depth of the Fire” [4:145]; concerning the People of the Table, “I shall punish him with a punishment with which I punish no other being” [5:115]; and “Admit Pharaoh’s people to the severest punishment” [40:46]. [Translator’s note: These descriptions of the unseen are presented in the source as reports.] The exception is real. Those who repent of hypocrisy, correct outward deeds and inward dispositions, trust in and attach themselves to God alone, and purify worship of ostentation and the wish for reputation, are joined to believers. Al-Farrāʾ takes “with the believers” as meaning “among the believers,” while al-ʿUtbī supposes the wording deliberately avoids saying “they are believers” to indicate God's displeasure. Ibn ʿAjība responds: “He says ‘with the believers,’ not ‘from among them,’ because escape from hypocrisy is difficult. One does not belong among the believers until freed from all its branches, and this is rare.” He cites the familiar Prophetic warning about lying when speaking, breaking promises and betraying trusts, even if a person fasts, prays and asserts Islam. Sincere people receive great reward, and the repentant share in it. God has no need to inflict punishment for personal gratification, since He is not harmed by sin or benefited by worship. Persisting in disbelief produces a spiritual disease; faith and thanksgiving cure it. Al-Bayḍāwī explains that gratitude precedes faith in the verse because a person first experiences benefits and offers rudimentary gratitude, then reflects until recognising the Giver. Al-Thaʿlabī instead takes the sequence as transposed: belief precedes gratitude, since gratitude without faith brings no saving benefit. God is Shākir , accepting slight deeds and repaying them many times over, and ʿAlīm , knowing the true degree of sincerity."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>No vice is harder for the self to uproot than ostentation: cut it back in one guise and it returns in another. It is fully purified only by effacement (<em>fanāʾ<\/em>) and complete absence from what is other than God. Ibn ʿAjība quotes al-Shushtarī:<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>With flowing tears, cleanse the eye<br>of witnessing all else: every sickness departs.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>A saying holds that sincerity never grows in the heart until one falls from people's gaze and lets people fall from one's own. Sincerity belongs to inward actions, whose reality is known to the Knower of the Unseen alone. Accordingly no one should simply declare another person ostentatious because a devotional act has been made visible. Ostentation can be present in secrecy and absent from public action. The <em>Ḥikam<\/em> warns that ostentation may enter even where no creature sees one. When the seeker is purified of its finer traces, restores the relationship with God and takes refuge in Him alone, he joins the sincerely devoted brought-near; his acts are valued and his endeavour gratefully received.<\/p>\n<p><strong>The marginal commentary cites the <em>Iḥyāʾ<\/em> and comments upon it.<\/strong> The hypocrites' lowest depth reflects denial after knowledge: the scholar's sin is aggravated because it knowingly violates what he understands. The marginal commentator connects this with the reported statement that, without the Prophet's intercession, Abū Ṭālib would have been in the lowest depth, on account of his refusal despite recognition. He also reports al-Ghazālī's explanation that open denial is one evil but concealed denial adds another, because the hypocrite values the judgment of people more than God's awareness of the heart. The severity of ostentation and hypocrisy thus concerns elevating human observation above divine observation; it is therefore graver than open unbelief. Here ends the material from the marginal commentary.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"No vice is harder for the self to uproot than ostentation: cut it back in one guise and it returns in another. It is fully purified only by effacement ( fanāʾ ) and complete absence from what is other than God. Ibn ʿAjība quotes al-Shushtarī: With flowing tears, cleanse the eye of witnessing all else: every sickness departs. A saying holds that sincerity never grows in the heart until one falls from people's gaze and lets people fall from one's own. Sincerity belongs to inward actions, whose reality is known to the Knower of the Unseen alone. Accordingly no one should simply declare another person ostentatious because a devotional act has been made visible. Ostentation can be present in secrecy and absent from public action. The Ḥikam warns that ostentation may enter even where no creature sees one. When the seeker is purified of its finer traces, restores the relationship with God and takes refuge in Him alone, he joins the sincerely devoted brought-near; his acts are valued and his endeavour gratefully received. The marginal commentary cites the Iḥyāʾ and comments upon it. The hypocrites' lowest depth reflects denial after knowledge: the scholar's sin is aggravated because it knowingly violates what he understands. The marginal commentator connects this with the reported statement that, without the Prophet's intercession, Abū Ṭālib would have been in the lowest depth, on account of his refusal despite recognition. He also reports al-Ghazālī's explanation that open denial is one evil but concealed denial adds another, because the hypocrite values the judgment of people more than God's awareness of the heart. The severity of ostentation and hypocrisy thus concerns elevating human observation above divine observation; it is therefore graver than open unbelief. Here ends the material from the marginal commentary."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4256,"arabic_characters":4129},{"id":"q4_cont_148","q":"4:148–149","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:148–149 · Wronged speech and divine pardon","source":"Cairo I:582","cairo":"I:582–583","source_page_first":582,"source_page_last":583,"ar":"لا يُحِبُّ اللَّهُ الْجَهْرَ بِالسُّوءِ مِنَ الْقَوْلِ إِلاَّ مَنْ ظُلِمَ وَكانَ اللَّهُ سَمِيعاً عَلِيماً (148) إِنْ تُبْدُوا خَيْراً أَوْ تُخْفُوهُ أَوْ تَعْفُوا عَنْ سُوءٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَفُوًّا قَدِيراً (149)\nقلت: (إلا من ظلم) : استثناء منقطع، أي: لكن من ظلم فلا بأس أن يشكو بظالمه ويدعو عليه، وليس المراد أن الله يحب ذلك منه، إذ العفو أحسن كما يقوله بعد، وقرئ: (إلا من ظَلَم) بالبناء للفاعل، أي: ولكن الظالم يفعل مالا يحبه الله.\nيقول الحق جلَ جلاله: لا يُحِبُّ اللَّهُ الْجَهْرَ أي: الإجهار بِالسُّوءِ مِنَ الْقَوْلِ لأنه مِن فِعل أهل الجفاء والجهل إِلَّا مَنْ ظُلِمَ فلا بأس أن يجهر بالدعاء على ظالمه، أو بالشكوى به. نظيرها: وَلَمَنِ انْتَصَرَ بَعْدَ ظُلْمِهِ فَأُولئِكَ مَا عَلَيْهِمْ مِنْ سَبِيلٍ. قال مجاهد: هذا في الضيف النازل إذا لم يُضَف ومُنِع حقه، أو أُسِيءَ قِراه، فقد رخص له أن يذكر ما صُنعَ به. وزَعَم أن ضيفا تضيّف قوما فأساءوا قراه، فاشتكاهم، فنزلت الآية رخصة في شكواه. وَكانَ اللَّهُ سَمِيعاً لدعاء المظلوم، ورده على الظالم، فلا يحتاج إلى جهره، عَلِيماً بالظالم فيعاقبه على قدر جرمه.\nثم رغَّب في العفو فقال: إِنْ تُبْدُوا خَيْراً: طاعة وبرًا كحسن الخلق ولين الجانب، أَوْ تُخْفُوهُ أي:\nتفعلوه سرًا، أَوْ تَعْفُوا عَنْ سُوءٍ بأن لا تؤاخذوا به من أساء إليكم، وهذا هو المقصود بالذكر، وإنما ذُكِرَ إبداءُ الخير وإخفاؤُه سببًا ووسيلة لذكره، ولذلك رتب عليه فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَفُوًّا قَدِيراً أي: كثير العفو عن العُصاة، مع كمال قدرته على الانتقام، فأنتم أولى بذلك، وهو حث للمظلوم على العفو، بعد ما رخَّص له في الانتصار، حملاً على مكارم الأخلاق.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن الباطن إذا كمل تطهيره وتحقق تنويره ظهر أثر ذلك على الظاهر من مكارم الأخلاق، ولين الجانب، وحسن الخطاب، وترك العتاب، فما كمن في غيب السرائر ظهر في شهادة الظواهر وما كمَن فيك ظَهَرَ على فيك، وهذه أخلاق الصوفية- رضى الله عنهم وأرضاهم- وبذلك وصفهم القائل فيهم، فقال:\nهيْنُون ليْنُون أيسَارٌ بنو يَسَرٍ ... سُوَّاسُ مَكرُمَةِ أبناءُ أيسَارِ\nلا يَنطقُون بغيرِ الحقِّ إن نطقُوا ... ولا يُمَاروُنَ إن مارَوُا بإكثَارِ\nمَن تَلق مِنهُم تَقُل هذاك سَيِّدهُم ... مِثلُ النُّجُومِ التي يُهدَى بها السَّارِ\nومن شأن الحضرة التهذيب والتأديب، فلا يبقى معها لغو ولا تأثيم، لأنها جنة معجلة، قال تعالى:\nلاَ يَسْمَعُونَ فِيها لَغْواً وَلا تَأْثِيماً إِلَّا قِيلًا سَلاماً سَلاماً.\nوأيضَا أهل الحضرة حصل لهم القرب من الحبيب، فهم في حضرة القريب على بساط القرب على الدوام، ولا يتصور منهم الجهر بالكلام، وهم في حضرة الملك العلاَّم. قال تعالى: وَخَشَعَتِ الْأَصْواتُ لِلرَّحْمنِ فَلا تَسْمَعُ إِلَّا هَمْساً، فرفعُ الصوت عند الصوفية مذموم شنيع، يدل على بُعد صاحبه كيف ما كان، وتأمل قضية الصِّدِّيق حيث قال له- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «مالك تقرأ سرًا؟» فقال: (إن الذي نناجيه ليس ببعيد) . أو كما قال، وإنما قال له صلى الله عليه وسلم: «ارفع قليلاً» إخراجًا له عن مُراده، تربية له. والله تعالى أعلم.\nولما قدّم أقبح الكفر، وهو كفر المنافقين، ذكر ما يليه، وهو كفر اليهود، فقال:","quran_ar":"لا يُحِبُّ اللَّهُ الْجَهْرَ بِالسُّوءِ مِنَ الْقَوْلِ إِلاَّ مَنْ ظُلِمَ وَكانَ اللَّهُ سَمِيعاً عَلِيماً (148) إِنْ تُبْدُوا خَيْراً أَوْ تُخْفُوهُ أَوْ تَعْفُوا عَنْ سُوءٍ فَإِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ عَفُوًّا قَدِيراً (149)","quran_en":"God does not love public utterance of evil speech, except where someone has suffered wrong. God is Hearing, Knowing. Whether you make goodness public or conceal it, or pardon an evil committed against you, God is Pardoning and Fully Powerful.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: “Except one wronged” is a disconnected exception: the one who suffers injustice is permitted to complain against or pray against the wrongdoer, but this does not mean God positively loves such speech, since forgiveness is better, as the next verse shows. In a variant reading <em>ẓalama<\/em> is active—“except the one who wronged”—that is, the offender engages in speech displeasing to God.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Except one wronged” is a disconnected exception: the one who suffers injustice is permitted to complain against or pray against the wrongdoer, but this does not mean God positively loves such speech, since forgiveness is better, as the next verse shows. In a variant reading ẓalama is active—“except the one who wronged”—that is, the offender engages in speech displeasing to God."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>God does not approve of crying out in evil words, an act of coarseness and ignorance. A person who has been oppressed may publicly complain or invoke judgment against the oppressor; 42:41 similarly excuses proportionate self-defence. Mujāhid applies it to a guest denied appropriate hospitality or badly treated, who may recount the treatment received. A report attributes its revelation to such a guest's complaint. God hears the prayer of the oppressed and his response to the oppressor —so raised speech is unnecessary—and knows the oppressor, whom He punishes in proportion to his wrongdoing.<\/p>\n<p>Yet the highest response is pardon. Showing goodness openly through virtue, gentleness and obedience, or performing it secretly, are valuable, but the point of the passage is pardon of the person who has wronged you. These other kinds of goodness prepare its mention. God is repeatedly forgiving, although possessed of perfect power to avenge; people should imitate that moral generosity. Thus, after granting the oppressed permission to protest, He encourages the more excellent character of pardon.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God does not approve of crying out in evil words, an act of coarseness and ignorance. A person who has been oppressed may publicly complain or invoke judgment against the oppressor; 42:41 similarly excuses proportionate self-defence. Mujāhid applies it to a guest denied appropriate hospitality or badly treated, who may recount the treatment received. A report attributes its revelation to such a guest's complaint. God hears the prayer of the oppressed and his response to the oppressor —so raised speech is unnecessary—and knows the oppressor, whom He punishes in proportion to his wrongdoing. Yet the highest response is pardon. Showing goodness openly through virtue, gentleness and obedience, or performing it secretly, are valuable, but the point of the passage is pardon of the person who has wronged you. These other kinds of goodness prepare its mention. God is repeatedly forgiving, although possessed of perfect power to avenge; people should imitate that moral generosity. Thus, after granting the oppressed permission to protest, He encourages the more excellent character of pardon."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Once inward purification and illumination are established, the outward character displays generosity, softness, gracious speech and an end to reproach. What lies in inward secrecy becomes manifest in outward conduct: what is hidden within appears upon the tongue. Ibn ʿAjība cites these lines about them:<\/p><blockquote><p>Easy and gentle, generous, sons of ease;<br>stewards of noble deeds, sons of the generous.<br>When they speak, they speak only truth;<br>nor, when they dispute, do they dispute at length.<br>Whomever you meet among them, you say, “That is their chief,”<br>like the stars by which the night traveller is guided.<\/p><\/blockquote><p> The divine Presence educates and refines: no empty or sinful speech remains there, like the Paradise where only “Peace, peace” is heard (56:25–26). Those continuously near the Beloved stand in the audience of the Most High; loud self-assertion does not fit that station. God says, “Voices are humbled before the All-Merciful, so you hear only a whisper” [20:108]. Among the Sufis, raising the voice is reprehensible and ugly, indicating the speaker’s remoteness whatever his condition. When the Prophet asked Abū Bakr why he recited so quietly, he is reported to have answered, “The One I address is not far away.” The Prophet instructed him to raise the voice a little, which Ibn ʿAjība interprets as training him not to become attached to his own chosen manner. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Once inward purification and illumination are established, the outward character displays generosity, softness, gracious speech and an end to reproach. What lies in inward secrecy becomes manifest in outward conduct: what is hidden within appears upon the tongue. Ibn ʿAjība cites these lines about them: Easy and gentle, generous, sons of ease; stewards of noble deeds, sons of the generous. When they speak, they speak only truth; nor, when they dispute, do they dispute at length. Whomever you meet among them, you say, “That is their chief,” like the stars by which the night traveller is guided. The divine Presence educates and refines: no empty or sinful speech remains there, like the Paradise where only “Peace, peace” is heard (56:25–26). Those continuously near the Beloved stand in the audience of the Most High; loud self-assertion does not fit that station. God says, “Voices are humbled before the All-Merciful, so you hear only a whisper” [20:108]. Among the Sufis, raising the voice is reprehensible and ugly, indicating the speaker’s remoteness whatever his condition. When the Prophet asked Abū Bakr why he recited so quietly, he is reported to have answered, “The One I address is not far away.” The Prophet instructed him to raise the voice a little, which Ibn ʿAjība interprets as training him not to become attached to his own chosen manner. God knows best."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2865,"arabic_characters":2727},{"id":"q4_cont_150","q":"4:150–151","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:150–151 · Refusing to distinguish between messengers","source":"Cairo I:583","cairo":"I:583–584","source_page_first":583,"source_page_last":584,"ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ يَكْفُرُونَ بِاللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِ وَيُرِيدُونَ أَنْ يُفَرِّقُوا بَيْنَ اللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِ وَيَقُولُونَ نُؤْمِنُ بِبَعْضٍ وَنَكْفُرُ بِبَعْضٍ وَيُرِيدُونَ أَنْ يَتَّخِذُوا بَيْنَ ذلِكَ سَبِيلاً (150) أُولئِكَ هُمُ الْكافِرُونَ حَقًّا وَأَعْتَدْنا لِلْكافِرِينَ عَذاباً مُهِيناً (151)\nقلت: (حقًا) : مصدر مؤكد للجملة، أو صفة لمصدر الكافرين، أي: كفروا كفرًا محققًا يقينًا. وأصل (أعتدنا) :\nأعددنا، أبدلت الدال تاء لقرب المخرج.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ يَكْفُرُونَ بِاللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِ وَيُرِيدُونَ أَنْ يُفَرِّقُوا بَيْنَ اللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِ بأن يؤمنوا بالله ويكفروا برسله، وَيَقُولُونَ نُؤْمِنُ بِبَعْضٍ الأنبياء وَنَكْفُرُ بِبَعْضٍ، كاليهود، آمنوا بموسى\nوعُزير والتوراة، وكفروا بعيسى ومحمد صلّى الله عليه وسلم، وَيُرِيدُونَ أَنْ يَتَّخِذُوا بَيْنَ ذلِكَ سَبِيلًا، أي: طريقًا وسطًا بين الإيمان والكفر، ولا واسطة، إذ الحق لا يختلف، فإن الإيمان بالله إنما يتم برسله وتصديقهم فيما بلغوا عنه، تفصيلاً وإجمالاً، فالكافر بالبعض كالكافر بالكل في الضلال. ولذلك حكم عليهم بصريح الكفر فقال: أُولئِكَ هُمُ الْكافِرُونَ حَقًّا أي: هم الكاملون في الكفر حقيقة، وإنما أكد كفرهم لأنهم تحكموا على الله، واتخذوا إلههم هواهم، حيث جعلوا الاختيار لهم دون الله، وفي ذلك منازعة للقدر، وتعطيل له، وهو كفر وشرك، ثم ذكر وعيدهم فقال: وَأَعْتَدْنا أي: هيأنا لِلْكافِرِينَ منهم عَذاباً مُهِيناً أي: يخزيهم ويهينهم، حين يُكرِّم أولياءَه ويرفع أقدارهم. جعلنا الله منهم. آمين.\nالإشارة: الأولياء على قَدم الأنبياء، فمن فرق بينهم حرم بركةَ جميعهم، ومن صدَّق بجميعهم وعَظَّمَهم اقتبس من أنوارهم كلهم، والله- تعالى- غيور على أوليائه، كما كان غيورًا على أنبيائه، فطرد من فرَّق بينهم، فكذلك يطرد من يقع في بعض أوليائه ويعظم البعض، لأن البعض هو الكل. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر من لم يفرق، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ يَكْفُرُونَ بِاللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِ وَيُرِيدُونَ أَنْ يُفَرِّقُوا بَيْنَ اللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِ وَيَقُولُونَ نُؤْمِنُ بِبَعْضٍ وَنَكْفُرُ بِبَعْضٍ وَيُرِيدُونَ أَنْ يَتَّخِذُوا بَيْنَ ذلِكَ سَبِيلاً (150) أُولئِكَ هُمُ الْكافِرُونَ حَقًّا وَأَعْتَدْنا لِلْكافِرِينَ عَذاباً مُهِيناً (151)","quran_en":"Those who disbelieve in God and His messengers and wish to distinguish between God and His messengers, saying, “We believe in some and reject others,” and seek a path in between—those are truly unbelievers. For those who deny, We have prepared a humiliating punishment.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>ḥaqqan<\/em> is an emphatic verbal noun reinforcing the whole judgment or qualifying an understood noun: they have disbelieved with real, certain unbelief. <em>Aʿtadnā<\/em> was originally <em>aʿdadnā<\/em>, the first dāl being replaced by tāʾ because their places of articulation are close. Those who affirm God but reject His messengers attempt an incoherent separation; the verse speaks especially, in Ibn ʿAjība's exposition, of Jews accepting Moses, Ezra and Torah but rejecting Jesus and Muḥammad. Such people seek an imagined middle way between belief and denial. Yet belief in God is completed by accepting the messengers and what they authentically communicate, each in the proper degree, in detail or generally. Denial of one legitimate messenger undermines the whole principle. He interprets the emphatic judgment as censuring those who made their own preferences an authority over God's choice, effectively treating whim as a deity and disputing divine decree. A humiliating punishment is contrasted with God's honouring and elevating of His friends. He prays that God include us with the latter.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: ḥaqqan is an emphatic verbal noun reinforcing the whole judgment or qualifying an understood noun: they have disbelieved with real, certain unbelief. Aʿtadnā was originally aʿdadnā , the first dāl being replaced by tāʾ because their places of articulation are close. Those who affirm God but reject His messengers attempt an incoherent separation; the verse speaks especially, in Ibn ʿAjība's exposition, of Jews accepting Moses, Ezra and Torah but rejecting Jesus and Muḥammad. Such people seek an imagined middle way between belief and denial. Yet belief in God is completed by accepting the messengers and what they authentically communicate, each in the proper degree, in detail or generally. Denial of one legitimate messenger undermines the whole principle. He interprets the emphatic judgment as censuring those who made their own preferences an authority over God's choice, effectively treating whim as a deity and disputing divine decree. A humiliating punishment is contrasted with God's honouring and elevating of His friends. He prays that God include us with the latter."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Saints follow in the footsteps of prophets. Ibn ʿAjība carries the verse over analogically: one who makes distinctions among the friends of God loses the blessing of their company, whereas one who respects them all receives illumination from each. God is jealous for His friends, as for His prophets. As He drove away those who distinguished between His prophets, so He drives away someone who disparages some of His saints while exalting others, for the part is the whole. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Saints follow in the footsteps of prophets. Ibn ʿAjība carries the verse over analogically: one who makes distinctions among the friends of God loses the blessing of their company, whereas one who respects them all receives illumination from each. God is jealous for His friends, as for His prophets. As He drove away those who distinguished between His prophets, so He drives away someone who disparages some of His saints while exalting others, for the part is the whole. God knows best."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1579,"arabic_characters":1676},{"id":"q4_cont_152","q":"4:152","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:152 · Those who believe in all messengers","source":"Cairo I:584","cairo":"I:584–585","source_page_first":584,"source_page_last":585,"ar":"وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بِاللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِ وَلَمْ يُفَرِّقُوا بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِنْهُمْ أُولئِكَ سَوْفَ نُؤْتِيهِمْ أُجُورَهُمْ وَكانَ اللَّهُ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً (152)\nقلت: (بين) : من الأمور النسبية، فلا بد أن تدخل على متعدد، تقول: جلست بين فلان وفلان، وإنما دخلت هنا على (أحد) لأنه يقتضي متعددًا لعمومه، لأنه وقع في سياق النفي. قاله البيضاوي.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بِاللَّهِ وما يجب له من الكمالات، (ورسله) وما يجب لهم كذلك، وَلَمْ يُفَرِّقُوا بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِنْهُمْ بأن آمنوا بجميعهم، وصدقوا بكل ما جاءوا به من عند ربهم، أُولئِكَ سَوْفَ نُؤْتِيهِمْ [١] أُجُورَهُمْ الموعودة لهم، بأن نُجِلَّ مقدارهم، ونرفع مقامهم، ونُبوئهم في جنات النعيم.\nوتصديره بسوف لتأكيد الوعد والدلالة على أنه كائن لا محالة وإن تأخر وقته، ولما كان العبد لا يخلو من نقص، رفع الخوف عنهم بقوله: وَكانَ اللَّهُ غَفُوراً لِما فرط منهم رَحِيماً بهم بتضعيف حسناتهم.\nالإشارة: والذين صدقوا بأولياء الله، وعظموا جميعهم، واقتبسوا من أنوارهم كلهم، أولئك سوف نؤتيهم أجورهم، بأن أُنعمهم في جنات المعارف في دار الدنيا، فإن ماتوا أسكنّاهم فى الفراديس العُلَى (في مَقْعَدِ صِدْقٍ عند مليك مقتدر) . والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر مساوئ اليهود فقال:","quran_ar":"وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بِاللَّهِ وَرُسُلِهِ وَلَمْ يُفَرِّقُوا بَيْنَ أَحَدٍ مِنْهُمْ أُولئِكَ سَوْفَ نُؤْتِيهِمْ أُجُورَهُمْ وَكانَ اللَّهُ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً (152)","quran_en":"Those who believe in God and His messengers and make no distinction among any of them—We shall give them their rewards. God is Forgiving, Merciful.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>bayna<\/em> (“between”) normally implies more than one referent, but here it governs “any one” (<em>aḥad<\/em>) because that word occurs in a negative context and therefore signifies the whole plurality. This is al-Bayḍāwī's explanation.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: bayna (“between”) normally implies more than one referent, but here it governs “any one” ( aḥad ) because that word occurs in a negative context and therefore signifies the whole plurality. This is al-Bayḍāwī's explanation."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>Those who affirm God's due perfections and the truthful mission and rank of His messengers without excluding one of them, believing what each communicated from the Lord, are promised a reward that raises and honours them in the gardens of bliss. The particle <em>sawfa<\/em> emphasises the promised reward's certainty, even if its appearance lies in the future. Because no servant is free of shortcomings, the verse adds that God is Forgiving of what they have neglected and Merciful in multiplying their good deeds.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Those who affirm God's due perfections and the truthful mission and rank of His messengers without excluding one of them, believing what each communicated from the Lord, are promised a reward that raises and honours them in the gardens of bliss. The particle sawfa emphasises the promised reward's certainty, even if its appearance lies in the future. Because no servant is free of shortcomings, the verse adds that God is Forgiving of what they have neglected and Merciful in multiplying their good deeds."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Those who acknowledge all genuine saints, honour them and benefit from their several illuminations receive their portion too. They will enjoy the gardens of divine knowledge in this world and, upon death, be placed in the high gardens, “in an abode of truth with a Sovereign fully powerful” (54:55). God knows best. The next passage returns to the People of the Book.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Those who acknowledge all genuine saints, honour them and benefit from their several illuminations receive their portion too. They will enjoy the gardens of divine knowledge in this world and, upon death, be placed in the high gardens, “in an abode of truth with a Sovereign fully powerful” (54:55). God knows best. The next passage returns to the People of the Book."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C584.1","page":584,"ar":"قرأ حفص عن عاصم (يؤتيهم) بالياء، وقرأ الباقون بالنون.","en":"Ḥafṣ, transmitting from ʿĀṣim, reads yuʾtīhim (\"He will give them\") with yāʾ; the other readers read with nūn (\"We will give them\").","anchor":"أُولئِكَ سَوْفَ نُؤْتِيهِمْ"}],"english_characters":1103,"arabic_characters":1111},{"id":"q4_cont_153","q":"4:153–154","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:153–154 · Scripture, Israel and the demand for signs","source":"Cairo I:585","cairo":"I:585–586","source_page_first":585,"source_page_last":586,"ar":"يَسْئَلُكَ أَهْلُ الْكِتابِ أَنْ تُنَزِّلَ عَلَيْهِمْ كِتاباً مِنَ السَّماءِ فَقَدْ سَأَلُوا مُوسى أَكْبَرَ مِنْ ذلِكَ فَقالُوا أَرِنَا اللَّهَ جَهْرَةً فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ الصَّاعِقَةُ بِظُلْمِهِمْ ثُمَّ اتَّخَذُوا الْعِجْلَ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ فَعَفَوْنا عَنْ ذلِكَ وَآتَيْنا مُوسى سُلْطاناً مُبِيناً (153) وَرَفَعْنا فَوْقَهُمُ الطُّورَ بِمِيثاقِهِمْ وَقُلْنا لَهُمُ ادْخُلُوا الْبابَ سُجَّداً وَقُلْنا لَهُمْ لا تَعْدُوا فِي السَّبْتِ وَأَخَذْنا مِنْهُمْ مِيثاقاً غَلِيظاً (154)\nقلت: من قرأ: (لا تعدوا) بالسكون، فماضيه: عدا، ومن قرأ بتشديد الدال، فماضيه اعتَدى، وأصله: لا تعتدوا، فنُقلت حركة التاء إلى العين وأدغمت التاء في الدال، ومن قرأ بالاختلاس أشار إلى الأصل.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يَسْئَلُكَ أَهْلُ الْكِتابِ، وهم أحبار اليهود، أَنْ تُنَزِّلَ عَلَيْهِمْ كِتاباً مِنَ السَّماءِ جملة واحدة، كما نزل التوراة، أو كتابًا بخطَّ سماوي على ألواح كما كانت التوراة، والسائل هو كعب بن الأشرف وفنحاص بن عازوراء وغيرهم، قالوا للنبى صلّى الله عليه وسلم: (إن كنت نبيًا فأتنا بكتاب من السماء جملةً، كما أتى به موسى) ، قال تعالى في الرد عليهم: فَقَدْ سَأَلُوا مُوسى أَكْبَرَ مِنْ ذلِكَ وهو رؤية ذات الحقّ- تعالى- جهرًا حسًا. والمعنى: إن استعظمت ما سألوا منك فقد وقع منهم ما هو أعظم من ذلك.\nوهذا السؤال، وإن كان من آبائهم، أُسند إليهم لأنهم كانوا آخذين بمذهبهم تابعين لهديهم، فما اقترحوا عليك ليس بأول جهالاتهم وتشغيبهم بل عُرفُهُم راسخٌ في ذلك، فلا تستغرب ما وقع منهم.\nثم فسر سؤالهم بقوله: فَقالُوا أَرِنَا اللَّهَ جَهْرَةً أي: عيانًا في الحس، فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ الصَّاعِقَةُ، بأن جاءت نار من السماء فأهلكتهم، فماتوا ثم بُعثوا بدعوة موسى عليه السلام وذلك بسبب ظلمهم. وهو تعنتهم وسؤالهم لما استحيل في تلك الحال التي كانوا عليها. وذلك لا يقتضي امتناع الرؤية مطلقًا. وسيأتي في الإشارة تحرير ذلك.\nثُمَّ اتَّخَذُوا الْعِجْلَ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ على وحدانيته تعالى. وهذه جناية أخرى اقترفها أيضًا أوائلهم، فَعَفَوْنا عَنْ ذلِكَ حين تابوا، ولم نعاجلهم بالعقوبة، وَآتَيْنا مُوسى سُلْطاناً مُبِيناً أي: تسلطًا ظاهرًا عليهم، حين أمرهم أن يقتلوا أنفسهم، توبة من اتخاذهم العجل إلهًا، وحجة واضحة على نبوته كالآيات التسع.\nوَرَفَعْنا فَوْقَهُمُ الطُّورَ حين امتنعوا من قبول أحكام التوراة، بسبب ميثاقهم الذي أخذناه عليهم، وهو التزام أحكام التوراة، وقلنا لهم على لسان موسى: ادْخُلُوا الْبابَ سُجَّداً أي: باب بيت المقدس، فدخلوا يزحفون على استاههم عنادًا واستهزاءً، وقلنا لهم: لا تَعْدُوا فِي السَّبْتِ على لسان داود عليه السلام، فاعتدوا فيه بالاصطياد، فمسخناهم قردةً وخنازير، وَأَخَذْنا مِنْهُمْ مِيثاقاً غَلِيظاً على ذلك كله، فنقضوا جميع ذلك، أو ميثاقًا غليظًا في التوراة لئن أدركوك ليؤمنن بك، وليبينن صفتك للناس، فنقضوا وكتموا. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: اقتراح الآيات وطلب الكرامات من الأولياء، سنة ماضية، لأنهم على قدم الأنبياء- عليهم السلام- ما يقال لهم إلا ما قيل للأنبياء قبلهم، فلا تكاد تجد أحدًا يصدق بولي حتى تظهر عليه الكرامة، وهو جهل كبير لأن الكرامة قد تظهر على من لم تكمل له استقامة، وقد تكون استدراجًا ومكرًا. وأيُّ كرامة أعظم من العلوم اللدنية والأخلاق النبوية؟ كما قال شيخنا رضي الله عنه. وقد ظهرت الكرامات على المتقدمين ولم ينقطع الإنكار عليهم.\nواعلم أن طلب الرؤية في الدنيا ليس بممتنع، وإنما عاقب الله بني إسرائيل على طلبها لأنهم طلبوها قبل إبانها، طلبوها من غير اتصاف بشروط حصولها، وهو كمال التهذيب والتطهير من دنس الحس، فمن كمل تهذيبه وتحقق تطهيره حصل له شهود الحق، حتى لو كُلف أن يشهد غيره لم يستطع، وذلك حين تستولي البصيرة على البصر، فيشهد البصر ما كانت تشهده البصيرة، وذلك بعد كمال فتحها. ولذلك قال في الحِكَم: «شعاعُ البصيرة يشهدك قرب الحق منك، وعين البصيرة يشهدك عدمك لوجوده، وحق البصيرة يشهدك وجود الحق» ... الخ كلامه. وهذه المشاهدة لا تحصل إلا لمن اتصل بشيخ التربية، وإلا فلا مطمع فيها. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر عقوبة اليهود حيث نقضوا العهود، فقال:","quran_ar":"يَسْئَلُكَ أَهْلُ الْكِتابِ أَنْ تُنَزِّلَ عَلَيْهِمْ كِتاباً مِنَ السَّماءِ فَقَدْ سَأَلُوا مُوسى أَكْبَرَ مِنْ ذلِكَ فَقالُوا أَرِنَا اللَّهَ جَهْرَةً فَأَخَذَتْهُمُ الصَّاعِقَةُ بِظُلْمِهِمْ ثُمَّ اتَّخَذُوا الْعِجْلَ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما جاءَتْهُمُ الْبَيِّناتُ فَعَفَوْنا عَنْ ذلِكَ وَآتَيْنا مُوسى سُلْطاناً مُبِيناً (153) وَرَفَعْنا فَوْقَهُمُ الطُّورَ بِمِيثاقِهِمْ وَقُلْنا لَهُمُ ادْخُلُوا الْبابَ سُجَّداً وَقُلْنا لَهُمْ لا تَعْدُوا فِي السَّبْتِ وَأَخَذْنا مِنْهُمْ مِيثاقاً غَلِيظاً (154)","quran_en":"The People of the Book ask you to bring down a book from heaven to them. They asked Moses for something even greater: “Show us God openly.” The thunderbolt seized them for their wrongdoing. Then, even after clear signs had come, they took the calf for worship. We pardoned that and granted Moses manifest authority. We raised the Mount over them on account of their covenant, ordered them to enter the gate bowing, and commanded them not to transgress on the Sabbath. We took from them a solemn covenant.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: one reading of <em>lā taʿdū<\/em> has a quiescent consonant and derives from <em>ʿadā<\/em>. Another doubles the <em>dāl<\/em>, deriving from <em>iʿtadā<\/em>: the original <em>lā taʿtadū<\/em> underwent a transfer of vowel and assimilation. A reading with an incompletely pronounced vowel preserves an indication of that origin.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: one reading of lā taʿdū has a quiescent consonant and derives from ʿadā . Another doubles the dāl , deriving from iʿtadā : the original lā taʿtadū underwent a transfer of vowel and assimilation. A reading with an incompletely pronounced vowel preserves an indication of that origin."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>Certain Jewish religious leaders, named as Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf and Finḥāṣ ibn ʿĀzūrāʾ, demanded that Muḥammad produce a complete heavenly book at once, or written celestial tablets as those associated with Moses. God responds that previous generations had demanded something more extraordinary: direct sensible vision of the divine Essence. The acts of their ancestors are addressed to these later claimants because they had adopted their inherited posture and participated in its defiance. Their rejection should not surprise the Prophet.<\/p>\n<p>Their forebears said, “Show us God openly,” seeking a sensory manifestation for which they had not been prepared. The thunderbolt, described as fire from heaven, destroyed them, and they were restored to life in response to Moses' supplication. Ibn ʿAjība insists this punishment does not mean the vision of God is altogether impossible; their request was untimely and wrong in its circumstances. Those forebears then worshipped the calf after proofs of divine unity had come. God accepted repentance and pardoned, bestowing on Moses manifest authority over them and evident proofs of prophecy, including the nine signs. Moses’ manifest authority over them is illustrated by his command that they kill themselves in repentance for taking the calf as a god.<\/p>\n<p>God raised Mount Sinai above them when they resisted the Torah's stipulations, obliging them through solemn covenant. They were told to enter the gate of the sacred city bowing, but in the traditional account they entered dragging themselves on their buttocks in obstinacy and mockery. They were forbidden to violate the Sabbath, through David, but transgressed by fishing; the text recalls the traditional narrative of punishment by transformation into apes and swine. A weighty covenant encompassed all these commands, which they broke; alternatively it included a stipulation that, should they live to meet Muḥammad, they would recognise and proclaim his traits, but they concealed them.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Certain Jewish religious leaders, named as Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf and Finḥāṣ ibn ʿĀzūrāʾ, demanded that Muḥammad produce a complete heavenly book at once, or written celestial tablets as those associated with Moses. God responds that previous generations had demanded something more extraordinary: direct sensible vision of the divine Essence. The acts of their ancestors are addressed to these later claimants because they had adopted their inherited posture and participated in its defiance. Their rejection should not surprise the Prophet. Their forebears said, “Show us God openly,” seeking a sensory manifestation for which they had not been prepared. The thunderbolt, described as fire from heaven, destroyed them, and they were restored to life in response to Moses' supplication. Ibn ʿAjība insists this punishment does not mean the vision of God is altogether impossible; their request was untimely and wrong in its circumstances. Those forebears then worshipped the calf after proofs of divine unity had come. God accepted repentance and pardoned, bestowing on Moses manifest authority over them and evident proofs of prophecy, including the nine signs. Moses’ manifest authority over them is illustrated by his command that they kill themselves in repentance for taking the calf as a god. God raised Mount Sinai above them when they resisted the Torah's stipulations, obliging them through solemn covenant. They were told to enter the gate of the sacred city bowing, but in the traditional account they entered dragging themselves on their buttocks in obstinacy and mockery. They were forbidden to violate the Sabbath, through David, but transgressed by fishing; the text recalls the traditional narrative of punishment by transformation into apes and swine. A weighty covenant encompassed all these commands, which they broke; alternatively it included a stipulation that, should they live to meet Muḥammad, they would recognise and proclaim his traits, but they concealed them."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Demand for signs and extraordinary miracles from saints is an old pattern. Saints walk in the prophets' footsteps and encounter analogous objections. People are often reluctant to acknowledge a saint without a <em>karāma<\/em>; yet extraordinary phenomena do not by themselves prove an established uprightness (<em>istiqāma<\/em>). A marvel can occur before a person is perfected, and can even be a trial or a deceptive elevation (<em>istidrāj<\/em>). Ibn ʿAjība quotes his shaykh: what miracle is greater than divinely granted knowledge (<em>ʿulūm ladunniyya<\/em>) and Prophetic virtues? Miracles appeared with earlier saints and did not end opposition to them.<\/p>\n<p>The desire to behold God during this life is not, in Ibn ʿAjība's inward interpretation, absolutely excluded. The Israelites were punished because they demanded it before its proper season, without the requisite perfection of training and purification from the defilement of sensory perception. When insight has been completely purified, it witnesses the Real so fully that it cannot be forced to witness anything other than Him. The inward eye comes to govern the outward eye, and the latter witnesses what the inward eye had first perceived. The <em>Ḥikam<\/em> is cited for three gradations: the ray of insight shows God's nearness; the eye of insight witnesses one's own non-existence before His existence; and the truth of insight witnesses the existence of the Real. Such witnessing is attained only by someone who has attached himself to a shaykh of spiritual training; otherwise there is no hope of it. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Demand for signs and extraordinary miracles from saints is an old pattern. Saints walk in the prophets' footsteps and encounter analogous objections. People are often reluctant to acknowledge a saint without a karāma ; yet extraordinary phenomena do not by themselves prove an established uprightness ( istiqāma ). A marvel can occur before a person is perfected, and can even be a trial or a deceptive elevation ( istidrāj ). Ibn ʿAjība quotes his shaykh: what miracle is greater than divinely granted knowledge ( ʿulūm ladunniyya ) and Prophetic virtues? Miracles appeared with earlier saints and did not end opposition to them. The desire to behold God during this life is not, in Ibn ʿAjība's inward interpretation, absolutely excluded. The Israelites were punished because they demanded it before its proper season, without the requisite perfection of training and purification from the defilement of sensory perception. When insight has been completely purified, it witnesses the Real so fully that it cannot be forced to witness anything other than Him. The inward eye comes to govern the outward eye, and the latter witnesses what the inward eye had first perceived. The Ḥikam is cited for three gradations: the ray of insight shows God's nearness; the eye of insight witnesses one's own non-existence before His existence; and the truth of insight witnesses the existence of the Real. Such witnessing is attained only by someone who has attached himself to a shaykh of spiritual training; otherwise there is no hope of it. God knows best."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3822,"arabic_characters":3579},{"id":"q4_cont_155","q":"4:155–158","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:155–158 · Covenant, transgression, and the Messiah","source":"Cairo I:586","cairo":"I:586–588","source_page_first":586,"source_page_last":588,"ar":"فَبِما نَقْضِهِمْ مِيثاقَهُمْ وَكُفْرِهِمْ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ وَقَتْلِهِمُ الْأَنْبِياءَ بِغَيْرِ حَقٍّ وَقَوْلِهِمْ قُلُوبُنا غُلْفٌ بَلْ طَبَعَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْها بِكُفْرِهِمْ فَلا يُؤْمِنُونَ إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً (155) وَبِكُفْرِهِمْ وَقَوْلِهِمْ عَلى مَرْيَمَ بُهْتاناً عَظِيماً (156) وَقَوْلِهِمْ إِنَّا قَتَلْنَا الْمَسِيحَ عِيسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ رَسُولَ اللَّهِ وَما قَتَلُوهُ وَما صَلَبُوهُ وَلكِنْ شُبِّهَ لَهُمْ وَإِنَّ الَّذِينَ اخْتَلَفُوا فِيهِ لَفِي شَكٍّ مِنْهُ ما لَهُمْ بِهِ مِنْ عِلْمٍ إِلاَّ اتِّباعَ الظَّنِّ وَما قَتَلُوهُ يَقِيناً (157) بَلْ رَفَعَهُ اللَّهُ إِلَيْهِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَزِيزاً حَكِيماً (158)\nقلت: (فبما) : صلة زيدت للتأكيد، و (نقضهم) : مصدر مجرور بالباء، وهي متعلقة بالفعل المحذوف، أي:\nبسبب نقضهم فَعَلنا بهم ما فعلنا، أو بقوله: (حرَمنا عليهم) ، ويكون (فبظلم) على هذا بدلاً من قوله: (فبما نقضهم) ، فيكون التحريم بسبب النقض، وما عطف عليه. والاستثناء في قوله: (إلا اتباع الظن) منقطع إذ العلم يناقض الظن.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: فلما أخذنا على بني إسرائيل العهد والميثاق خالفوا ونقضوا، ففعلنا بهم ما فعلنا، بسبب نقضهم ميثاقهم، أو بسبب نقضهم وكفرهم حَرَّمْنا عَلَيْهِمْ طَيِّباتٍ أُحِلَّتْ لَهُمْ، وبسبب كفرهم أيضًا بِآياتِ اللَّهِ القرآن، أو بما في كتبهم، وَقَتْلِهِمُ الْأَنْبِياءَ بِغَيْرِ حَقٍّ وَقَوْلِهِمْ قُلُوبُنا غُلْفٌ أي: مغلفة لا تفقه ما تقول.\nقال تعالى في الرد عليهم: بَلْ طَبَعَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْها بِكُفْرِهِمْ، فجعلها محجوبة عن العلم، بأن خذلها ومنعها التوفيق للتدبر في الآيات والتذكر بالمواعظ، فَلا يُؤْمِنُونَ إِلَّا قَلِيلًا منهم كعبد الله بن سلام وأصحابه، أو إيمانًا قليلاً لا عبرة به لنقصانه، وَبِكُفْرِهِمْ أيضًا بعيسى عاقبناهم وطبعنا على قلوبهم، وَقَوْلِهِمْ عَلى مَرْيَمَ بُهْتاناً عَظِيماً أي: نسبتها للزنى وبقولهم: إِنَّا قَتَلْنَا الْمَسِيحَ عِيسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ رَسُولَ اللَّهِ أي بزعمه، ويحتمل أنهم قالوه استهزاء، ونظيره: إِنَّ رَسُولَكُمُ الَّذِي أُرْسِلَ إِلَيْكُمْ لَمَجْنُونٌ، أو يكون استئنافًا من الله بمدحه، أو وضعًا للذكر الحسن موضع قولهم القبيح. قاله البيضاوي.\nثم رد الله تعالى عليهم فقال: وَما قَتَلُوهُ وَما صَلَبُوهُ وَلكِنْ شُبِّهَ لَهُمْ، رُوِي أنَّ رهطاً من اليهود سبوه هو وأمه، فدعا عليهم، فمُسخوا قردة وخنازير، فاجتمعت اليهود على قتله، فقال لهم: يا معشر اليهود، إن الله يبغضكم، فغضبوا وثاروا ليقتلوه، فبعث الله تعالى جبريل فأدخله خوخة فيها كُوة في سقفها، ورفعه الله إلى السماء من تلك الكوة، فأمر اليهود رجلاً منهم يقال له: طيطانوس، أن يدخل الخوخة ويقتله، فلما دخل الخوخة، لم ير عيسى، فألقى الله تعالى شبه عيسى عليه، فلما أبطأ عليهم دخلوا عليه، فظنوه عيسى، فقتلوه وصلبوه.\nوقال قتادة: ذكر لنا أن عيسى عليه السلام قال لأصحابه: أيكم يقذف عليه شبهي فيقتل؟ فقال رجل: أنا يا رسول الله، فقتل ذلك الرجل، ورفع عيسى عليه السلام، وكساه الريش وألبسه النور، وقطع عنه لذة المطعم والمشرب وصار مع الملائكة، فهو معهم في السماء إنسيًّا ملكيًّا، أرضيًّا سماويًّا.\nوَإِنَّ الَّذِينَ اخْتَلَفُوا فِيهِ لَفِي شَكٍّ مِنْهُ فقال بعض اليهود: إن كان هذا عيسى فأين صاحبنا؟ وإن كان صاحبنا فأين عيسى؟ ويقال: إن الله تعالى ألقى شبه وجه عيسى على صاحبهم، ولم يلق عليه شبه جسده، فلما\nقتلوه ونظروا إليه، فقالوا: الوجه وجه عيسى والجسد جسد صاحبنا. ما لَهُمْ بِهِ مِنْ عِلْمٍ إِلَّا اتِّباعَ الظَّنِّ أي:\nلا علم لهم بقتله، لكن يتبعون الظن فقط. وَما قَتَلُوهُ قتلاً يَقِيناً كما زعموا بقولهم: إنا قتلنا المسيح، بَلْ رَفَعَهُ اللَّهُ إِلَيْهِ فهو في السماء الثانية مع يحيى عليها السلام، وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَزِيزاً حَكِيماً أي: قويًا بالنقمة على اليهود، حكيمًا فيما حكم عليهم من اللعنة والغضب. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: نقضُ عهود الشيوخ من أسباب المقت والبعد عن الله، وكذلك الإنكار عليهم والطعن فيهم، وكذلك البعد عن وعظهم وتذكيرهم، وضد هذا من موجبات القرب والحب من الله، كحفظ حرمتهم، والوقوف مع أوامرهم، والذب عنهم حين تهتك حرمتهم، والدنو منهم، والسعي في خدمتهم. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم ذكر نزول عيسى فى آخر الزمان، فقال:","quran_ar":"فَبِما نَقْضِهِمْ مِيثاقَهُمْ وَكُفْرِهِمْ بِآياتِ اللَّهِ وَقَتْلِهِمُ الْأَنْبِياءَ بِغَيْرِ حَقٍّ وَقَوْلِهِمْ قُلُوبُنا غُلْفٌ بَلْ طَبَعَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْها بِكُفْرِهِمْ فَلا يُؤْمِنُونَ إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً (155) وَبِكُفْرِهِمْ وَقَوْلِهِمْ عَلى مَرْيَمَ بُهْتاناً عَظِيماً (156) وَقَوْلِهِمْ إِنَّا قَتَلْنَا الْمَسِيحَ عِيسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ رَسُولَ اللَّهِ وَما قَتَلُوهُ وَما صَلَبُوهُ وَلكِنْ شُبِّهَ لَهُمْ وَإِنَّ الَّذِينَ اخْتَلَفُوا فِيهِ لَفِي شَكٍّ مِنْهُ ما لَهُمْ بِهِ مِنْ عِلْمٍ إِلاَّ اتِّباعَ الظَّنِّ وَما قَتَلُوهُ يَقِيناً (157) بَلْ رَفَعَهُ اللَّهُ إِلَيْهِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَزِيزاً حَكِيماً (158)","quran_en":"For their breaking of the covenant, denying God's signs, killing prophets without right, and saying, “Our hearts are covered”—rather, God sealed their hearts because of their unbelief, so that few believe; and for their denial and their monstrous slander against Mary; and for saying, “We killed the Messiah, Jesus son of Mary, the Messenger of God.” They did not kill him and did not crucify him, but it was made to appear so to them. Those who differ concerning him are in doubt, possessing no knowledge beyond following conjecture. They certainly did not kill him; rather God raised him to Himself. God is Mighty, Wise.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>mā<\/em> in <em>fa-bi-mā naqḍihim<\/em> strengthens the expression. “Their breaking” is a verbal noun governed by <em>bi-<\/em> and related either to a suppressed verb—“because they broke the covenant, We dealt with them as We did”—or to the later “We prohibited good things for them”; in the latter parsing the “because of the wrongdoing” of 4:160 restates the cause, with the prohibitions following covenant-breaking and its associated offences. “Except following conjecture” is a disconnected exception because genuine knowledge and conjecture are opposed.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: mā in fa-bi-mā naqḍihim strengthens the expression. “Their breaking” is a verbal noun governed by bi- and related either to a suppressed verb—“because they broke the covenant, We dealt with them as We did”—or to the later “We prohibited good things for them”; in the latter parsing the “because of the wrongdoing” of 4:160 restates the cause, with the prohibitions following covenant-breaking and its associated offences. “Except following conjecture” is a disconnected exception because genuine knowledge and conjecture are opposed."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>Israel's former commitments were broken, so consequences followed. Ibn ʿAjība includes the later removal of previously permitted good things, the denial of divine signs in Scripture or Qurʾān, the unjust killing of prophets, and the claim “Our hearts are wrapped,” as though unable to receive the Prophet's message. God answers that their continued denial has veiled the hearts from understanding and withheld success in contemplating signs and accepting admonition. “Few believe” may refer to a minority such as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his associates; alternatively it means a meagre faith too deficient to count. He also cites rejection of Jesus, the monstrous accusation of sexual misconduct made against Mary, and the boast of killing Jesus, called “the Messenger of God” either mockingly by the speakers or by God as an insertion honouring Jesus amid their words, as al-Bayḍāwī explains.<\/p>\n<p>In Ibn ʿAjība's theological telling, God decisively denies that they killed or crucified Jesus. He recounts one narrative in which hostile Jews insulted Jesus and his mother; after Jesus prayed against them, they were transformed into apes and swine, and the Jews assembled to kill him. He told them, “Company of Jews, God hates you.” They became angry and rose to kill him. Gabriel led Jesus into an enclosure with an opening in its roof, through which God lifted him. An attacker named Ṭīṭānūs was sent inside; God caused him to resemble Jesus, and when the others entered they mistook him for Jesus and killed and crucified him. A different account attributed to Qatāda reports that Jesus asked his companions who would receive his resemblance and be killed; a volunteer accepted, Jesus was raised, clothed in feathers and in light <em>[Translator’s note: The close earlier version in al-Thaʿlabī’s commentary, III:410, also places him among angels circling the Throne. This context supports “feathers” for <i>rīsh<\/i>; “fine apparel” remains a lexical alternative. This preference concerns the narrative’s wording, not its historical authenticity.]<\/em>, and deprived of the pleasure of food and drink, living among angels in heaven, human and angelic, earthly and heavenly.<\/p>\n<p>Those who disputed about the death were uncertain. Some wondered how the figure could be Jesus if their companion was missing, or their companion if Jesus had escaped. Another report says only the substitute's face, not his body, resembled Jesus, provoking their suspicion. They followed conjecture, not certainty. God, says Ibn ʿAjība, raised Jesus to heaven, where the commentator places him in the second heaven with John. God is mighty in His vengeance upon the Jews and wise in His judgment of curse and anger upon them. <em>[Translator’s note: The competing substitution accounts are transmitted exegetical narratives. Their historical particulars are additional to the Qurʾānic phrase <i>shubbiha lahum<\/i>, “it was made to appear so to them.”]<\/em><\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Israel's former commitments were broken, so consequences followed. Ibn ʿAjība includes the later removal of previously permitted good things, the denial of divine signs in Scripture or Qurʾān, the unjust killing of prophets, and the claim “Our hearts are wrapped,” as though unable to receive the Prophet's message. God answers that their continued denial has veiled the hearts from understanding and withheld success in contemplating signs and accepting admonition. “Few believe” may refer to a minority such as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his associates; alternatively it means a meagre faith too deficient to count. He also cites rejection of Jesus, the monstrous accusation of sexual misconduct made against Mary, and the boast of killing Jesus, called “the Messenger of God” either mockingly by the speakers or by God as an insertion honouring Jesus amid their words, as al-Bayḍāwī explains. In Ibn ʿAjība's theological telling, God decisively denies that they killed or crucified Jesus. He recounts one narrative in which hostile Jews insulted Jesus and his mother; after Jesus prayed against them, they were transformed into apes and swine, and the Jews assembled to kill him. He told them, “Company of Jews, God hates you.” They became angry and rose to kill him. Gabriel led Jesus into an enclosure with an opening in its roof, through which God lifted him. An attacker named Ṭīṭānūs was sent inside; God caused him to resemble Jesus, and when the others entered they mistook him for Jesus and killed and crucified him. A different account attributed to Qatāda reports that Jesus asked his companions who would receive his resemblance and be killed; a volunteer accepted, Jesus was raised, clothed in feathers and in light [Translator’s note: The close earlier version in al-Thaʿlabī’s commentary, III:410, also places him among angels circling the Throne. This context supports “feathers” for rīsh ; “fine apparel” remains a lexical alternative. This preference concerns the narrative’s wording, not its historical authenticity.] , and deprived of the pleasure of food and drink, living among angels in heaven, human and angelic, earthly and heavenly. Those who disputed about the death were uncertain. Some wondered how the figure could be Jesus if their companion was missing, or their companion if Jesus had escaped. Another report says only the substitute's face, not his body, resembled Jesus, provoking their suspicion. They followed conjecture, not certainty. God, says Ibn ʿAjība, raised Jesus to heaven, where the commentator places him in the second heaven with John. God is mighty in His vengeance upon the Jews and wise in His judgment of curse and anger upon them. [Translator’s note: The competing substitution accounts are transmitted exegetical narratives. Their historical particulars are additional to the Qurʾānic phrase shubbiha lahum , “it was made to appear so to them.”]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Breaking commitments made to spiritual guides is among the causes of God’s abhorrence and of distance from Him, as can hostile denunciation of them and refusal of their admonitions. The contrary acts—respecting their dignity, abiding by their commands, defending them against slander, maintaining proper nearness and serving appropriately—are portrayed as means to proximity and love. <em>[Translator’s note: This is Ibn ʿAjība’s application of the passage to spiritual formation. It does not establish prophetic infallibility or unlimited immunity for human teachers.]<\/em> God gives success.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Breaking commitments made to spiritual guides is among the causes of God’s abhorrence and of distance from Him, as can hostile denunciation of them and refusal of their admonitions. The contrary acts—respecting their dignity, abiding by their commands, defending them against slander, maintaining proper nearness and serving appropriately—are portrayed as means to proximity and love. [Translator’s note: This is Ibn ʿAjība’s application of the passage to spiritual formation. It does not establish prophetic infallibility or unlimited immunity for human teachers.] God gives success."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":4024,"arabic_characters":3635},{"id":"q4_cont_159","q":"4:159","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:159 · The People of the Book and Jesus","source":"Cairo I:588","cairo":"I:588–589","source_page_first":588,"source_page_last":589,"ar":"وَإِنْ مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ إِلاَّ لَيُؤْمِنَنَّ بِهِ قَبْلَ مَوْتِهِ وَيَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ يَكُونُ عَلَيْهِمْ شَهِيداً (159)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَإِنْ مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ أي: ما من يهودي ولا نصراني، أي: الموجودين حين نزوله إِلَّا لَيُؤْمِنَنَّ بعيسى قَبْلَ مَوْتِهِ أي: عيسى، وذلك حين نزوله من السماء، رُوِيَ أنه ينزل من السماء حين يخرج الدجال فيهلكه، ولا يبقى أحد من أهل الكتاب إلا ويؤمن به، حتى تكون الملة واحدة، وهي ملة الإسلام، وتقع الأمنة حتى يرتع الأسود مع الإبل، والنمور مع البقر، والذئاب مع الغنم، ويلعب الصبيان بالحيات، ويلبث في الأرض أربعين سنة، ثم يتوفّى ويصلى عليه المسلمون ويدفنونه.\nوقيل الضمير فى (به) إلى عيسى، وفي (موته) إلى الكتابي، أي: وإن من أهل الكتاب أحد إلا ليؤمنن بعيسى بأنه عبد الله ورسوله، قَبْلَ مَوْتِهِ أي: قبل خروج نفس ذلك الكتابي إذا عاين الملك، فلا ينفعه حينئذ إيمانه، لأن كل من نزل به الموت لم تخرج نفسه حتى يتبين له الحق من الباطل. ويؤيد هذا قراءة من قرأ: ليُؤمنُنَّ به قبل موتهم بضم النون، لأن (أحدًا) في معنى الجمع، وهذا كالوعيد لهم والتحريض على معاجلة الإيمان به من قبل أن يُضطر إليه ولم ينفعه إيمانه، وَيَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ يَكُونُ عَلَيْهِمْ شَهِيداً يشهد على اليهود بالتكذيب، وعلى النصارى بأنهم دعوه ابن الله. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: عند الموت تتحقق الحقائق، ويتميز الحق من الباطل، ويحصل الندم، ولا ينفع حين تزل القدم، فالمطلوب المبادرة بتحقيق الإيمان، وتحصيل مقام العرفان، قبل أن يسقط إلى جنبه، فينفرد رهينًا في قبره بذنبه.\nوالله تعالى أعلم.\nثم ذكر وبال ظلمهم وعدوانهم فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِنْ مِنْ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ إِلاَّ لَيُؤْمِنَنَّ بِهِ قَبْلَ مَوْتِهِ وَيَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ يَكُونُ عَلَيْهِمْ شَهِيداً (159)","quran_en":"There is none among the People of the Book who will not believe in him before his death, and on the Day of Resurrection he will be a witness against them.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>On the first reading, every Jew and Christian living at Jesus' descent will believe in him before Jesus' death. Ibn ʿAjība transmits a traditional account in which Jesus descends when the Antichrist appears, destroys him, and all the surviving People of the Book recognise Jesus, leaving one religious community of Islam. Peace becomes so complete that lions graze with camels, leopards with cattle and wolves with sheep, while children safely play with snakes. Jesus remains for forty years, dies, receives the Muslim funeral prayer and is buried. <em>[Translator’s note: The timescale and illustrative details belong to the report quoted by the commentator.]<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A second reading makes “his death” refer to each member of the People of the Book rather than Jesus: every such person will acknowledge before their own death that Jesus is God's servant and messenger, upon seeing the angel of death and learning truth from error. This final compulsory recognition, Ibn ʿAjība says, cannot replace faith freely attained earlier. He mentions a reading in which the pronoun denotes the plurality of those dying, and understands the passage as warning them to believe before death. On the Day of Resurrection Jesus will bear witness that some denied him and others ascribed divine sonship to him.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"On the first reading, every Jew and Christian living at Jesus' descent will believe in him before Jesus' death. Ibn ʿAjība transmits a traditional account in which Jesus descends when the Antichrist appears, destroys him, and all the surviving People of the Book recognise Jesus, leaving one religious community of Islam. Peace becomes so complete that lions graze with camels, leopards with cattle and wolves with sheep, while children safely play with snakes. Jesus remains for forty years, dies, receives the Muslim funeral prayer and is buried. [Translator’s note: The timescale and illustrative details belong to the report quoted by the commentator.] A second reading makes “his death” refer to each member of the People of the Book rather than Jesus: every such person will acknowledge before their own death that Jesus is God's servant and messenger, upon seeing the angel of death and learning truth from error. This final compulsory recognition, Ibn ʿAjība says, cannot replace faith freely attained earlier. He mentions a reading in which the pronoun denotes the plurality of those dying, and understands the passage as warning them to believe before death. On the Day of Resurrection Jesus will bear witness that some denied him and others ascribed divine sonship to him."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Death exposes realities and distinguishes truth from error, often bringing remorse when the opportunity for purposeful change has passed. The aim is to attain established faith and inward recognition (<em>ʿirfān<\/em>) now, before the foot slips and the person is left alone, held in pledge in his grave by his sin. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Death exposes realities and distinguishes truth from error, often bringing remorse when the opportunity for purposeful change has passed. The aim is to attain established faith and inward recognition ( ʿirfān ) now, before the foot slips and the person is left alone, held in pledge in his grave by his sin. God knows best."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1606,"arabic_characters":1420},{"id":"q4_cont_160","q":"4:160–161","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:160–161 · Wrongdoing, usury and forbidden things","source":"Cairo I:589","cairo":"I:589","source_page_first":589,"source_page_last":589,"ar":"فَبِظُلْمٍ مِنَ الَّذِينَ هادُوا حَرَّمْنا عَلَيْهِمْ طَيِّباتٍ أُحِلَّتْ لَهُمْ وَبِصَدِّهِمْ عَنْ سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ كَثِيراً (160) وَأَخْذِهِمُ الرِّبَوا وَقَدْ نُهُوا عَنْهُ وَأَكْلِهِمْ أَمْوالَ النَّاسِ بِالْباطِلِ وَأَعْتَدْنا لِلْكافِرِينَ مِنْهُمْ عَذاباً أَلِيماً (161)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: فبسبب ظلم مِنَ الَّذِينَ هادُوا وهو نقضهم الميثاق، وكفرهم بآيات الله، وقتلهم الأنبياء، حَرَّمْنا عَلَيْهِمْ طَيِّباتٍ كانت أُحِلَّتْ لَهُمْ كالشحوم، وكل ذي ظفر، وغير ذلك من لذيذ الطيبات، وكانوا كلما ارتكبوا كبيرة حرّم عليهم شيئًا من الطيّبات، وحرّمنا ذلك أيضًا عليهم بِصَدِّهِمْ عن طريق اللَّهِ صدًّا كَثِيراً، أي: بإعراضهم عنه إعراضًا كثيرًا، أو بصدهم عنه ناسًا كثيرًا كانوا يُخَذِّلونَهُم عن الدخول في دين الله، وبأخذهم الربا وَقَدْ نُهُوا عَنْهُ، فهو محرم عليهم وعلى الأمة المحمدية، وبأكلهم أَمْوالَ النَّاسِ بِالْباطِلِ كالرشوة وما كانوا يأخذونه من عوامهم، وَأَعْتَدْنا لِلْكافِرِينَ مِنْهُمْ بمحمد صلى الله عليه وسلم عَذاباً أَلِيماً، دون من تاب وآمن به.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن كل غفلة ومعصية وسوء أدب يحرم مرتكبه بسببه من لذيذ الطاعات وحلاوة المشاهدات على قدره، شعر أو لم يشعر، وقد يبعده من الحضرة وهو لا يشعر، مكرًا واستدراجًا، فإذا أصر عليه سلب من مقام الولاية بالكلية، ولا يزال ينقص إيمانه شيئًا فشيئًا، حتى يتفلت منه، والعياذ بالله، وإذا بادر بالتوبة رجى قبوله، وكل يقظة وطاعة وحسن أدب يوجب لصاحبه الزلفى والقرب من الحضرة، ويزيده في حلاوة المعاملة والمشاهدة على قدره، فلا يزال يتقرب إليه بنوافل الخيرات، حتى يحبه فيتولاه، فيكون سمعه وبصره، كما في الحديث.\nوبالله التوفيق.\nثم استثنى من تاب من اليهود، فقال:","quran_ar":"فَبِظُلْمٍ مِنَ الَّذِينَ هادُوا حَرَّمْنا عَلَيْهِمْ طَيِّباتٍ أُحِلَّتْ لَهُمْ وَبِصَدِّهِمْ عَنْ سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ كَثِيراً (160) وَأَخْذِهِمُ الرِّبَوا وَقَدْ نُهُوا عَنْهُ وَأَكْلِهِمْ أَمْوالَ النَّاسِ بِالْباطِلِ وَأَعْتَدْنا لِلْكافِرِينَ مِنْهُمْ عَذاباً أَلِيماً (161)","quran_en":"For wrongdoing among those who became Jews, We forbade them good things previously permitted, and because they often obstructed God's way, took usury although forbidden it, and consumed people's wealth without right. For the deniers among them We have prepared a painful punishment.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>Because of covenant-breaking, denial of God's signs and the murder of prophets, Ibn ʿAjība says, food formerly permitted was prohibited to the Children of Israel: kinds of fat, clawed animals and other desirable foods. In his account, whenever they committed a grave sin, He prohibited some good thing to them. They also turned from the divine way themselves or obstructed many other people from entering it, accepted usury (<em>ribā<\/em>) despite its prohibition in their own dispensation, and took wealth unjustly through bribes and illegitimate demands upon ordinary people. Usury is prohibited in the Muḥammadan dispensation as well. The punishment falls upon those who persist in denial, not those who repent and truly believe.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Because of covenant-breaking, denial of God's signs and the murder of prophets, Ibn ʿAjība says, food formerly permitted was prohibited to the Children of Israel: kinds of fat, clawed animals and other desirable foods. In his account, whenever they committed a grave sin, He prohibited some good thing to them. They also turned from the divine way themselves or obstructed many other people from entering it, accepted usury ( ribā ) despite its prohibition in their own dispensation, and took wealth unjustly through bribes and illegitimate demands upon ordinary people. Usury is prohibited in the Muḥammadan dispensation as well. The punishment falls upon those who persist in denial, not those who repent and truly believe."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Every heedlessness, sin and breach of spiritual courtesy (<em>sūʾ al-adab<\/em>) deprives its perpetrator, to a corresponding degree, of the sweetness of worship and the delights of witnessing, whether the person notices or not. One may be distanced from divine presence without realising it, in an imperceptible spiritual trial. If he persists, he is stripped of the station of protecting friendship (<i>wilāya<\/i>) altogether, and his faith continues to weaken little by little until it escapes him. God preserve us. Immediate repentance offers hope of restoration. Conversely, every awakening, obedient act and refinement of conduct brings a person nearer and increases the sweetness of devotional work and direct vision. By supererogatory good deeds a servant draws near until God loves him and becomes his hearing and sight, as the famous divine saying relates. God gives success.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Every heedlessness, sin and breach of spiritual courtesy ( sūʾ al-adab ) deprives its perpetrator, to a corresponding degree, of the sweetness of worship and the delights of witnessing, whether the person notices or not. One may be distanced from divine presence without realising it, in an imperceptible spiritual trial. If he persists, he is stripped of the station of protecting friendship ( wilāya ) altogether, and his faith continues to weaken little by little until it escapes him. God preserve us. Immediate repentance offers hope of restoration. Conversely, every awakening, obedient act and refinement of conduct brings a person nearer and increases the sweetness of devotional work and direct vision. By supererogatory good deeds a servant draws near until God loves him and becomes his hearing and sight, as the famous divine saying relates. God gives success."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1597,"arabic_characters":1506}];
